1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,060
On the morning of June tenth, nineteen twelve,

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,919
a mother in southwest Iowa

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,140
telephoned a neighbor's house.

4
00:00:08,839 --> 00:00:10,000
Her husband told the coroner about it.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,160
My wife did, yes.

6
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,339
He could not say what time.

7
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:17,080
She tried, he said,

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,360
about three different times.

9
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:22,500
Her two daughters had stayed the night there.

10
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:24,379
She expected them back.

11
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:26,539
That house was locked.

12
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:27,580
The blinds were down.

13
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:30,480
Everyone who tells this story

14
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,240
will tell you why it was never solved.

15
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,560
A crowd walked through the rooms.

16
00:00:36,759 --> 00:00:37,560
The evidence was lost.

17
00:00:38,259 --> 00:00:41,140
And that is partly true.

18
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:44,179
But here is what the paperwork shows.

19
00:00:44,899 --> 00:00:47,460
This case did not run out of evidence.

20
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,240
For nineteen years

21
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:51,079
it kept getting more.

22
00:00:51,759 --> 00:00:52,359
A closet.

23
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:56,740
A photograph, said to come from inside a child's eye.

24
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,719
A sound that turned into a man.

25
00:01:00,399 --> 00:01:01,539
A signed confession.

26
00:01:02,219 --> 00:01:02,960
A fingerprint.

27
00:01:03,579 --> 00:01:07,140
I can't tell you who killed eight people in Villisca.

28
00:01:07,879 --> 00:01:08,560
No one can.

29
00:01:09,239 --> 00:01:11,400
What I can give you is an inventory.

30
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:12,680
What was in that house.

31
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:15,739
And what was carried in afterward.

32
00:01:16,375 --> 00:01:18,135
The first entry is the people.

33
00:01:18,694 --> 00:01:19,995
Joe Moore was forty-three.

34
00:01:20,534 --> 00:01:21,894
He sold farm implements.

35
00:01:22,474 --> 00:01:23,995
Sarah Moore was thirty-nine,

36
00:01:24,534 --> 00:01:27,174
and she helped run the Children's Day program

37
00:01:27,795 --> 00:01:29,194
at the Presbyterian church.

38
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:30,534
Herman was eleven.

39
00:01:31,094 --> 00:01:32,015
That Sunday night,

40
00:01:32,575 --> 00:01:33,394
he spoke a piece

41
00:01:33,974 --> 00:01:36,894
about God watching over the sparrows.

42
00:01:37,454 --> 00:01:38,055
Katherine was ten.

43
00:01:38,234 --> 00:01:38,875
Boyd was seven.

44
00:01:39,414 --> 00:01:40,254
Paul was five.

45
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:44,894
Lena Stillinger took part in the program too.

46
00:01:45,795 --> 00:01:46,754
Her sister Ina was with her.

47
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:48,094
The records disagree about their ages.

48
00:01:48,694 --> 00:01:52,115
Lena was eleven or twelve.

49
00:01:52,575 --> 00:01:53,694
Ina was eight or nine,

50
00:01:54,234 --> 00:01:56,055
and one trial report says six.

51
00:01:56,594 --> 00:01:58,855
At six o'clock that evening,

52
00:01:59,414 --> 00:02:00,694
according to their older sister Blanche,

53
00:02:01,234 --> 00:02:03,075
Joe Moore telephoned the Stillinger home.

54
00:02:03,655 --> 00:02:08,254
He did not want the girls walking back in the dark.

55
00:02:08,495 --> 00:02:10,275
Could they stay with his family?

56
00:02:10,875 --> 00:02:14,055
Blanche said she thought that would be all right.

57
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:16,694
The pastor testified the program ended about nine fifteen.

58
00:02:17,034 --> 00:02:18,694
Nobody who saw what came next

59
00:02:19,215 --> 00:02:22,694
ever gave an account that held.

60
00:02:23,074 --> 00:02:25,314
So I won't give you one.

61
00:02:25,916 --> 00:02:26,556
Monday morning.

62
00:02:27,216 --> 00:02:29,116
A neighbor, Mary Peckham,

63
00:02:29,816 --> 00:02:30,736
noticed how still the house was.

64
00:02:31,016 --> 00:02:32,136
She tried the doors.

65
00:02:32,856 --> 00:02:35,716
Joe's brother Ross came with a key.

66
00:02:36,136 --> 00:02:39,616
He opened the door to the room off the parlor.

67
00:02:40,316 --> 00:02:41,056
He told the coroner

68
00:02:41,756 --> 00:02:44,496
he did not wait long enough to see anything else.

69
00:02:45,216 --> 00:02:48,476
The town marshal, Hank Horton, went through every room.

70
00:02:49,156 --> 00:02:50,596
And then he did something

71
00:02:51,296 --> 00:02:52,096
almost nobody retells.

72
00:02:52,096 --> 00:02:53,256
According to the Moore store clerk,

73
00:02:54,096 --> 00:02:55,816
he locked the house

74
00:02:56,596 --> 00:03:00,436
and left the keys with the neighbor.

75
00:03:01,116 --> 00:03:01,716
Remember that.

76
00:03:00,256 --> 00:03:01,676
A locked house.

77
00:03:02,116 --> 00:03:04,616
A woman next door, holding the key.

78
00:03:05,156 --> 00:03:08,676
It is the last moment anyone could say

79
00:03:09,356 --> 00:03:10,316
who had been inside.

80
00:03:11,096 --> 00:03:12,116
When the doctors went in,

81
00:03:12,796 --> 00:03:13,896
every face was covered.

82
00:03:14,696 --> 00:03:15,576
Dr. Williams climbed the stairs

83
00:03:15,856 --> 00:03:19,216
to the room on the south side.

84
00:03:20,016 --> 00:03:21,896
A small boy, on his stomach.

85
00:03:22,676 --> 00:03:23,696
A gauze undershirt

86
00:03:24,456 --> 00:03:26,076
laid over the top of his head.

87
00:03:26,796 --> 00:03:30,916
Dr. Williams told the inquest what he did next.

88
00:03:31,816 --> 00:03:32,756
I lifted that off

89
00:03:33,656 --> 00:03:34,396
lifted it off

90
00:03:35,296 --> 00:03:36,516
to see which one it was.

91
00:03:42,208 --> 00:03:43,708
So here is the first inventory.

92
00:03:44,148 --> 00:03:48,688
An ax, near the bed where Lena and Ina were found.

93
00:03:49,148 --> 00:03:51,768
A piece of bacon, wrapped in a flour sack,

94
00:03:52,268 --> 00:03:55,228
at the head of that bed.

95
00:03:55,668 --> 00:03:57,888
A lamp on the floor, at the foot of the parents' bed.

96
00:03:58,308 --> 00:04:02,328
The marshal moved it out of the way.

97
00:04:02,728 --> 00:04:04,608
Skirts and aprons hung over the windows.

98
00:04:05,068 --> 00:04:06,048
Marks in the ceiling.

99
00:04:06,488 --> 00:04:07,608
Dr. Williams found no footprints.

100
00:04:08,048 --> 00:04:09,268
Five years later,

101
00:04:09,568 --> 00:04:10,368
the coroner testified

102
00:04:10,808 --> 00:04:12,148
there were no finger marks on the ax handle.

103
00:04:12,608 --> 00:04:15,168
Then the house was opened again.

104
00:04:15,568 --> 00:04:18,028
The National Guard came to hold the line.

105
00:04:18,448 --> 00:04:19,548
One account says ten thirty.

106
00:04:19,968 --> 00:04:21,888
Another says noon.

107
00:04:22,308 --> 00:04:25,548
How many people went in before that, I can't tell you.

108
00:04:25,808 --> 00:04:27,888
Later accounts say one hundred.

109
00:04:28,348 --> 00:04:30,188
Others say hundreds.

110
00:04:30,608 --> 00:04:31,708
I have not found a count from that day.

111
00:04:32,168 --> 00:04:33,988
What the record does show

112
00:04:34,228 --> 00:04:36,448
is a house entered again and again.

113
00:04:37,008 --> 00:04:40,668
Jessie Moore testified she went in that day,

114
00:04:41,108 --> 00:04:42,088
to get family pictures for the press.

115
00:04:42,528 --> 00:04:43,648
Bedding was burned.

116
00:04:44,268 --> 00:04:46,048
The house was cleaned, then repapered.

117
00:04:46,488 --> 00:04:47,228
So yes.

118
00:04:47,688 --> 00:04:48,328
Things were lost.

119
00:04:48,328 --> 00:04:49,808
But loss only explains what disappeared.

120
00:04:50,488 --> 00:04:52,808
It cannot explain what showed up.

121
00:04:53,541 --> 00:04:57,181
Item one arrives five days after the murders.

122
00:04:57,621 --> 00:04:58,681
A newspaper report, datelined Omaha.

123
00:04:59,201 --> 00:05:00,641
A woman named Retta Johnson

124
00:05:01,081 --> 00:05:02,841
has come back from Villisca.

125
00:05:03,281 --> 00:05:06,181
There is a closet, she says,

126
00:05:06,721 --> 00:05:09,581
off the room where the Stillinger girls slept.

127
00:05:10,101 --> 00:05:11,921
Bags of cotton batting inside.

128
00:05:12,381 --> 00:05:15,081
On them, the marks of a man

129
00:05:15,461 --> 00:05:16,621
who had sat and stood there.

130
00:05:17,061 --> 00:05:18,641
And in the girls' bed,

131
00:05:19,081 --> 00:05:21,881
a piece of watch chain.

132
00:05:22,301 --> 00:05:23,401
Now follow the closet.

133
00:05:23,841 --> 00:05:24,701
By the trial, five years on,

134
00:05:25,101 --> 00:05:29,601
the list of exhibits names a closet upstairs.

135
00:05:30,061 --> 00:05:32,681
A public comment posted under historian Edgar Epperly's name

136
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:37,341
traces a version of the story to a Kansas City reporter,

137
00:05:37,561 --> 00:05:40,041
with cigarettes, and a heel print in the dust.

138
00:05:40,621 --> 00:05:42,941
It says an undertaker checked,

139
00:05:43,681 --> 00:05:48,021
and the box of cotton sat on two suit boxes

140
00:05:48,561 --> 00:05:49,301
that had not been crushed.

141
00:05:49,601 --> 00:05:52,521
Today, you will hear it was the attic.

142
00:05:52,901 --> 00:05:53,701
Now follow the chain.

143
00:05:54,161 --> 00:05:58,961
A watch chain, in a bed, in June.

144
00:05:59,381 --> 00:06:00,241
By the trial,

145
00:06:00,681 --> 00:06:02,521
a piece of key chain,

146
00:06:02,921 --> 00:06:05,761
and Joe Moore's watch, on the floor.

147
00:06:06,221 --> 00:06:07,881
The containers stayed the same.

148
00:06:08,561 --> 00:06:10,881
What was inside them kept changing.

149
00:06:11,499 --> 00:06:12,779
August, nineteen twelve.

150
00:06:13,499 --> 00:06:17,279
A man from Villisca tells people in Council Bluffs

151
00:06:17,999 --> 00:06:19,899
that detectives have a photograph of the killer.

152
00:06:20,599 --> 00:06:22,419
They got it, he says,

153
00:06:22,819 --> 00:06:25,879
from the retina of one of the Stillinger sisters.

154
00:06:26,599 --> 00:06:30,419
I have found no such photograph in any record.

155
00:06:31,119 --> 00:06:34,799
Her parents were alive to read that.

156
00:06:35,559 --> 00:06:37,199
By the end of that year,

157
00:06:38,099 --> 00:06:42,899
newspapers had reported at least five different men held or arrested.

158
00:06:43,179 --> 00:06:43,779
A brother-in-law.

159
00:06:43,779 --> 00:06:45,839
A man with stained shoes.

160
00:06:46,039 --> 00:06:49,659
A farmhand who wrote about a vision.

161
00:06:50,379 --> 00:06:52,999
A Sioux City porter,

162
00:06:53,679 --> 00:06:55,819
whom the headline identified by his race.

163
00:06:56,079 --> 00:06:57,019
A farmer.

164
00:06:57,019 --> 00:06:59,799
None of them was convicted of anything at Villisca.

165
00:07:00,459 --> 00:07:06,319
The question had stopped being what happened in the house.

166
00:07:06,599 --> 00:07:07,759
It had become

167
00:07:08,479 --> 00:07:11,399
who was going to pay for it.

168
00:07:12,291 --> 00:07:13,751
In nineteen sixteen,

169
00:07:14,151 --> 00:07:15,291
a private detective named James Wilkerson

170
00:07:15,591 --> 00:07:16,891
brought the case a theory.

171
00:07:17,271 --> 00:07:18,411
State senator Frank Jones,

172
00:07:18,851 --> 00:07:22,291
Joe Moore's old employer and business rival,

173
00:07:22,691 --> 00:07:23,751
had hired a killer.

174
00:07:24,171 --> 00:07:24,951
Wilkerson's man was William Mansfield.

175
00:07:25,371 --> 00:07:28,011
A grand jury refused to indict him.

176
00:07:28,451 --> 00:07:30,351
Frank Jones was never charged with anything.

177
00:07:30,791 --> 00:07:32,711
He sued Wilkerson for slander,

178
00:07:33,051 --> 00:07:33,651
asked for sixty thousand dollars,

179
00:07:33,991 --> 00:07:35,891
and lost.

180
00:07:36,291 --> 00:07:37,991
And in that lawsuit,

181
00:07:38,411 --> 00:07:40,611
a witness named Ed Landers took the oath.

182
00:07:41,091 --> 00:07:41,751
You have met Landers already,

183
00:07:42,011 --> 00:07:43,751
in a way.

184
00:07:44,291 --> 00:07:46,891
He was staying a few houses from the Moores.

185
00:07:47,311 --> 00:07:49,651
At the inquest in nineteen twelve,

186
00:07:50,071 --> 00:07:51,771
he described something he heard that Sunday night.

187
00:07:52,171 --> 00:07:54,271
A sound.

188
00:07:54,771 --> 00:07:56,691
Like one boy hooting for another.

189
00:07:57,071 --> 00:07:59,851
The next day, he said, it occurred to him

190
00:08:00,511 --> 00:08:03,131
it might have been a woman moaning.

191
00:08:03,551 --> 00:08:05,651
In the published summary of that testimony,

192
00:08:06,071 --> 00:08:07,171
that is all there is.

193
00:08:07,631 --> 00:08:09,251
Four years later,

194
00:08:09,711 --> 00:08:10,311
under oath,

195
00:08:10,871 --> 00:08:14,111
he said that on that same night

196
00:08:14,491 --> 00:08:15,091
he saw Frank Jones's son, Albert,

197
00:08:15,311 --> 00:08:18,371
go into the Moore home.

198
00:08:19,771 --> 00:08:21,271
Albert Jones was never charged either.

199
00:08:21,691 --> 00:08:23,331
Both men denied any part in it.

200
00:08:23,751 --> 00:08:27,611
The prosecution attacked Landers's account in open court.

201
00:08:28,051 --> 00:08:28,851
A sound

202
00:08:29,751 --> 00:08:30,431
became a man.

203
00:08:36,083 --> 00:08:39,803
In nineteen seventeen, the state found its own document.

204
00:08:40,363 --> 00:08:41,063
The Reverend Lyn George Kelly

205
00:08:41,263 --> 00:08:42,603
was a traveling preacher,

206
00:08:43,003 --> 00:08:45,983
in town for one Sunday.

207
00:08:46,403 --> 00:08:47,963
He was charged with the murder of Lena Stillinger.

208
00:08:48,203 --> 00:08:52,123
He had been charged with mailing obscene letters,

209
00:08:52,563 --> 00:08:55,483
and he had been held in a federal mental hospital.

210
00:08:55,903 --> 00:08:57,903
He caught the five nineteen train that morning.

211
00:08:58,623 --> 00:09:01,483
The train left hours before anyone opened the door.

212
00:09:01,803 --> 00:09:03,363
Witnesses swore he spoke of the murders

213
00:09:03,803 --> 00:09:06,103
before the bodies were found.

214
00:09:06,523 --> 00:09:07,983
They swore it five years later.

215
00:09:08,503 --> 00:09:12,523
A station ticket record backed up the date of one encounter.

216
00:09:12,803 --> 00:09:15,623
It could not record what was said.

217
00:09:16,043 --> 00:09:16,643
Then, Logan jail.

218
00:09:16,683 --> 00:09:17,303
August thirtieth.

219
00:09:17,723 --> 00:09:18,523
A newspaper editor testified

220
00:09:18,923 --> 00:09:22,483
that he was handcuffed and placed in Kelly's cell,

221
00:09:22,763 --> 00:09:24,103
playing an automobile thief.

222
00:09:24,523 --> 00:09:25,763
A sheriff read out accusations.

223
00:09:26,183 --> 00:09:27,903
The editor recalled eighty-seven paragraphs.

224
00:09:28,363 --> 00:09:29,343
Kelly said,

225
00:09:29,643 --> 00:09:31,083
that's a lie.

226
00:09:31,523 --> 00:09:34,643
Early the next morning, a county attorney took down a statement.

227
00:09:35,043 --> 00:09:36,863
Its first lines say it was made

228
00:09:37,623 --> 00:09:39,343
without any promise or threats.

229
00:09:39,763 --> 00:09:43,763
In it, a voice tells Kelly to slay utterly.

230
00:09:44,063 --> 00:09:46,883
He climbs the stairs, thinking they are Jacob's ladder.

231
00:09:47,203 --> 00:09:48,743
He kills the children first, it says.

232
00:09:49,183 --> 00:09:51,163
Then the parents.

233
00:09:51,583 --> 00:09:54,143
No record I have read can tell you

234
00:09:54,563 --> 00:09:57,443
whether that is the order it happened.

235
00:09:58,163 --> 00:09:59,583
The attorney general told the jury

236
00:10:00,003 --> 00:10:02,883
Kelly had been asked fifteen times

237
00:10:03,303 --> 00:10:04,803
whether he killed the parents first.

238
00:10:05,263 --> 00:10:07,463
And fifteen times, he said no.

239
00:10:07,863 --> 00:10:11,243
Twenty-five times, he was told no promise could be made.

240
00:10:11,643 --> 00:10:14,983
Fifty-one times, he was told to tell the truth.

241
00:10:15,443 --> 00:10:16,503
The state counted those.

242
00:10:16,943 --> 00:10:20,623
It offered them as proof that he was free.

243
00:10:21,003 --> 00:10:21,603
At trial, Kelly said

244
00:10:21,983 --> 00:10:26,463
I have no recollection of signing that paper.

245
00:10:26,703 --> 00:10:27,663
The record shows a signature.

246
00:10:28,363 --> 00:10:31,823
It cannot show you what the signature is worth.

247
00:10:32,583 --> 00:10:35,603
There is one more name in the Omaha paper.

248
00:10:36,343 --> 00:10:38,643
A committee was raising money for Kelly's defense.

249
00:10:39,423 --> 00:10:40,283
It held meetings.

250
00:10:41,003 --> 00:10:41,763
It passed the hat.

251
00:10:42,503 --> 00:10:43,823
Its officers are listed.

252
00:10:44,583 --> 00:10:46,483
Remember the man who told the coroner

253
00:10:47,223 --> 00:10:48,303
my wife did, yes.

254
00:10:49,043 --> 00:10:50,983
She phoned about three different times.

255
00:10:51,723 --> 00:10:52,523
The state of Iowa had charged Kelly

256
00:10:52,763 --> 00:10:53,643
with killing his daughter.

257
00:10:54,403 --> 00:10:55,443
And Joseph Stillinger

258
00:10:56,843 --> 00:10:59,963
was the president of the committee

259
00:11:00,703 --> 00:11:01,543
trying to set him free.

260
00:11:07,208 --> 00:11:08,608
The paper does not say why.

261
00:11:09,088 --> 00:11:12,768
The committee said it wanted the guilty man brought to justice.

262
00:11:13,228 --> 00:11:16,188
It did not believe it had him.

263
00:11:16,628 --> 00:11:19,248
The first jury took twenty ballots.

264
00:11:19,768 --> 00:11:20,488
Eleven for acquittal, every time.

265
00:11:20,928 --> 00:11:26,688
One man, W. C. Brown, held for not guilty by reason of insanity.

266
00:11:27,148 --> 00:11:30,328
In November, a second jury acquitted Kelly.

267
00:11:30,788 --> 00:11:31,748
He was found not guilty.

268
00:11:32,008 --> 00:11:33,888
That is the verdict.

269
00:11:34,488 --> 00:11:35,908
There is one last item.

270
00:11:36,348 --> 00:11:37,968
March, nineteen thirty-one.

271
00:11:38,368 --> 00:11:40,528
A man in a Detroit jail,

272
00:11:40,968 --> 00:11:42,648
after five hours with detectives,

273
00:11:43,108 --> 00:11:44,088
confesses to the Villisca murders.

274
00:11:44,088 --> 00:11:45,828
The papers report

275
00:11:46,328 --> 00:11:50,568
that his fingerprints matched prints found at the scene.

276
00:11:50,988 --> 00:11:51,588
Remember the coroner.

277
00:11:50,268 --> 00:11:53,548
No finger marks on the handle.

278
00:11:53,988 --> 00:11:55,528
And this man's confession has a problem.

279
00:11:55,928 --> 00:11:58,148
He says he killed a man,

280
00:11:58,588 --> 00:11:59,188
his wife,

281
00:11:59,668 --> 00:12:02,188
and their four children.

282
00:12:02,588 --> 00:12:03,208
Six people.

283
00:12:03,928 --> 00:12:06,048
Officers said it did not tally.

284
00:12:06,748 --> 00:12:08,148
The last confession in the file

285
00:12:09,048 --> 00:12:11,128
forgot Lena and Ina.

286
00:12:16,791 --> 00:12:18,851
So here is the inventory now.

287
00:12:19,591 --> 00:12:20,231
A house.

288
00:12:21,011 --> 00:12:21,611
An ax.

289
00:12:22,331 --> 00:12:23,211
A few photographs.

290
00:12:23,951 --> 00:12:25,351
Everything else was carried in.

291
00:12:26,111 --> 00:12:29,891
Some of it, by people with something to gain.

292
00:12:30,331 --> 00:12:32,351
Much of it, I think,

293
00:12:32,671 --> 00:12:37,591
by people who could not stand an empty line on the form.

294
00:12:38,351 --> 00:12:39,571
That is what this kind of crime does.

295
00:12:40,071 --> 00:12:43,951
It leaves a space the exact size of a name.

296
00:12:44,691 --> 00:12:48,871
And a town will put anything in it.

297
00:12:49,631 --> 00:12:50,231
A stranger.

298
00:12:50,531 --> 00:12:51,131
A senator.

299
00:12:50,931 --> 00:12:52,831
A preacher who heard voices.

300
00:12:53,291 --> 00:12:56,711
No one has ever been convicted of these murders.

301
00:12:57,131 --> 00:12:57,971
Reverend Kelly was acquitted.

302
00:12:58,431 --> 00:13:01,631
Frank and Albert Jones were never charged.

303
00:13:01,991 --> 00:13:03,731
The one thing in this file

304
00:13:04,011 --> 00:13:06,271
that nobody needed to invent

305
00:13:06,991 --> 00:13:07,891
is a mother,

306
00:13:08,671 --> 00:13:09,271
on a Monday morning,

307
00:13:09,151 --> 00:13:10,331
calling a house.

308
00:13:11,231 --> 00:13:12,731
About three different times.

309
00:13:12,731 --> 00:13:18,591
This film is drawn from court testimony and newspapers of the time.

310
00:13:19,031 --> 00:13:20,371
A report is not proof.

311
00:13:20,851 --> 00:13:23,811
Everyone named here is presumed innocent of these murders.
